            "**Targeting** then chooses the segments that are largest, most at risk, most reachable and most likely to respond, since resources are finite; and **positioning** decides how the product should be perceived relative to the competition.",
            "**Process of a social marketing programme:** consumer and market research; segmentation and targeting; setting behavioural objectives; designing the marketing mix; developing and **pre-testing** the product, packaging and message; establishing the distribution channel and training the retailers and workers; launching with promotion; continuous monitoring of sales, coverage and consumer response; and evaluation with redesign.",
            "**Evaluation:** sales or offtake and its trend; coverage and reach in the target segment; brand recall; correct use of the product; behaviour change measured against a baseline; equity of uptake across segments; cost per unit sold and cost per behaviour changed; and, where the scale allows, health impact.",
